    "question": {
      ":question/id": "c5d411bc-7dfb-5946-972c-70b34d77650c",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}-(-2)=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=2$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(\\frac{5}{2})-(2)=3.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=3-(\\frac{5}{2})-(-2)=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{2}$, ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}$, and ${x}_{3}=2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\u27e8\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5}{2},2\u27e9.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
